  "worked_solution": "From the slope field, we see that the slope is zero whenever $x=-3$ or $y=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/10ae8ed1fe49b32938dba062ad13e39ce1339c6ac9b94d67f679ab60b288be00.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $y=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=(x+3)(y-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-3,$ $b=3,$ and $2a-b=2(-3)-3=-9.$",
